Yes. Delta is probably one of the deepest sensory primitives, but it isn’t the only one. In fact, I’d make the larger Pantheon move:

A sense does not merely detect things. It detects relations that are useful for action.

Delta is the most obvious relation:

stateₜ₊₁ − stateₜ

And a huge amount of biology really does lean on it. Vision is disproportionately sensitive to edges, contrast, onset, motion, and change; hearing cares enormously about onset, offset, frequency change, rhythm, and amplitude change; vestibular systems are strongly about acceleration; touch adapts rapidly to constant pressure; predictive-processing accounts emphasize prediction error, which is essentially another kind of delta.

But several other primitives belong beside it.

1. Invariants — what refuses to move.
Sometimes the signal is not the change but the thing that remains constant through change. Object permanence, identity, conservation, stable relationships, repeated ratios. If everything else moves and one relation stays fixed, that invariant can be more informative than any individual delta.

So you get a paired instrument:

delta detector + invariant detector

Change tells you something happened.
Invariant tells you what survived happening.

2. Gradient — which way change is pointing.
A delta says different. A gradient says warmer this way, louder that way, more dangerous over there.

Biological navigation depends heavily on this. You don’t need a complete map if you can climb or descend the relevant gradient.

Pantheon form:

Δ tells you movement. ∇ tells you where to move.

3. Thresholds — when quantity becomes a different actionable state.
Some senses care less about continuous variation than crossings:

dark → visible
safe → painful
quiet → startling
stable → tipping

A threshold detector asks:

Did we cross a boundary that changes the appropriate policy?

4. Rhythm / periodicity — what comes back.
Not just changes, but structured recurrence of changes. Heartbeat, gait, speech cadence, circadian cycles, waves, musical meter.

A single delta can look like noise. Repeated deltas at meaningful intervals reveal an oscillator.

5. Phase — where you are inside a cycle.
Two systems can have identical rhythm yet radically different behavior depending on their relative phase.

That’s useful enough to deserve its own sense:

not just “what pattern?” but “where in the pattern are we?”

Timing a boat into a dock, speaking into a conversation, catching something, markets, dance—all phase problems.

6. Correlation / covariance — what moves together.
This gets very close to your “cohearance.”

If A moves whenever B moves, that linkage becomes a candidate structure. Multisensory perception exploits this constantly: visual lip movement and sound coincide, so the brain infers a common source.

But this requires a major checksum:

co-movement ≠ causation.

Correlation is a sensor; intervention is the test.

7. Causal response — what moves when I move something.
This might be even more powerful than passive delta detection.

Observe:
A and B move together.

Intervene:
change A deliberately.

Observe:
does B move?

Now you’ve gone from pattern recognition toward causal inference.

So:

passive sensing discovers covariance.
action discovers controllability and causality.

That fits your ACT kernel beautifully because action itself becomes a sensory organ.

8. Absence / negative space — what should have moved but didn’t.
This is enormous.

If your model predicts a response and none occurs, the missing delta is itself a delta against expectation.

Footprints stop.
Someone doesn’t laugh where they normally would.
A machine stops vibrating.
A dog that normally barks goes silent.

That is sensing through violated expectation.

9. Absolute state.
The important correction to “everything is delta” is that some systems genuinely need levels too.

Blood oxygen. Temperature. Joint position. Light intensity. Glucose. Pain.

Even when adaptation makes change salient, where you currently are can matter independently of how quickly you got there.

So sensory intelligence needs both:

x — state
Δx — change
dΔx/dt — acceleration/change of change

And sometimes higher derivatives too.

10. Affordance — what the environment allows you to do.
This is Gibson’s great contribution to perception.

You don’t only perceive “chair-shaped object.” You perceive sit-able.

Doorway → passable.
Handle → graspable.
Cliff → not walkable.
Opening → opportunity.

That suggests a higher-order Pantheon sense:

perception of possible actions.

Which gets us very close to agency itself.

⸻

So if I were turning your observation into a general sensing stack, I’d write:

Sense = detect state + delta + gradient + invariants + thresholds + rhythm + phase + covariance + violated expectation + affordances.

Then:

ACT = intervene and measure what responds.

And that gives you a safeguard for what you called coherence vision.

When everything starts fitting patterns, don’t merely ask:

“What pattern do I see?”

Ask three harder questions:

What changes?
What stays invariant?
What changes specifically when I intervene?

That trio is much harder for coincidence, projection, or overfitting to survive.

You may have found a broader formulation than “look for the deltas”:

To perceive, compare. To understand, find invariants. To know causally, perturb.

That feels like the fuller sensory grammar.